= Appendix D — Calculations and Conventions

== D.1 Food labels

Multiply the amount per serving by the number of servings consumed. If a product contains 6 g per serving and three servings are eaten, the total is 6 × 3 = 18 g. Half a serving contains 3 g. Compare products on the same serving basis and check the units. A label total is not a medical diagnosis or a complete picture of a diet.

== D.2 Time and proportions

Four 15-minute sessions total 60 minutes. A sanitizer containing at least 60% alcohol is the CDC-recommended alternative when soap and water are unavailable; it may be less effective on visibly dirty or greasy hands. Follow current local guidance and product instructions.

== D.3 Risk

A risk factor changes probability; it does not prove that a person has a disease or will develop one. Associations alone do not establish the cause of every individual case. A qualified professional can interpret tests and symptoms in context.
